{"title":"KVPY","category":"Engineering","bank":"kvpy","subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Hydrogen","count":7,"questions":[{"id":51246,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Hydrogen","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"<p>In water-gas shift reaction, hydrogen gas is produced from the reaction of steam with.</p>","options":["  methane","  coke","  carbon dioxide<br />\n ","carbon monoxide"],"answer":3,"solution":"Water-gas shift reaction is<br />\n<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>CO</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mfrac><mrow><mi>FeO</mi><mo>·</mo><msub><mi>Cr</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>(</mo><mi>Catalyst</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow/></mfrac><mo>></mo><mo> </mo><msub><mi>CO</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math><br />\nIn this reaction, hydrogen gas is produced from the reaction of steam with carbon dioxide.","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2019 (SA)","_marksId":"614da48639e5582089effc6d"},{"id":51247,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Hydrogen","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"Treatment with lime can remove hardness of water caused by","options":["<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>CaCl</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>CaSO</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></math>"," <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Ca</mi><msub><mfenced><msub><mi>HCO</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mfenced><mn>2</mn></msub></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>CaCO</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math>"],"answer":2,"solution":"Temporary hardness (caused by bicarbonates of calcium or magnesium) can be removed by using lime, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Ca</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>OH</mi><msub><mo>)</mo><mn>2</mn></msub></math><br />\n<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mtable columnalign=\"right\"><mtr><mtd><mi>Ca</mi><msub><mfenced><msub><mi>HCO</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mfenced><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>Ca</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>OH</mi><msub><mo>)</mo><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>⟶</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>CaCO</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd/></mtr></mtable></math>","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2019 (SA)","_marksId":"614da486cb97470db630c4c4"},{"id":51248,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Hydrogen","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"Strength of a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> solution is labeled as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>79</mn><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi></math>. Its strength can also be expressed as closest to","options":["<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>20</mn></math> volume","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>5</mn></math> volume","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>10</mn></math> volume","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>15</mn></math> volume"],"answer":2,"solution":"<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mfenced><mrow><mi>aq</mi><mo>.</mo></mrow></mfenced></mrow></msub><mo>⟶</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℓ</mi></mfenced><mo>+</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi></mfenced></math></p>\n\n<p>General Solution :- </p>\n\n<p>It is a common trend that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi></math>-factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mfenced></math> is<br />\ntaken as '<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn></math>' </p>\n\n<p>By the definition of volume strength of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> if we consume <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mi>ℓ</mi></math> of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mo> </mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> in the above equation we are using 1 gram equivalent of<br />\n<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≡</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn></math> moles of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> (by using <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi></math>-factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></math>)</p>\n\n<p>This will produce <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac></math> moles of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> gas at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mo>.</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi><mo>.</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mo>.</mo></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>≡</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac><mo>×</mo><mn>22</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>6</mn><mi>ℓ</mi></math> of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> gas</p>\n\n<p>i.e. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mi>ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mo> </mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> solution gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>5</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>6</mn><mi>ℓ</mi></math> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mo>.</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi><mo>.</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></math></p>\n\n<p>Hence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mo>≡</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>6</mn></math> 'vol.' <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> solution</p>\n\n<p>In the given question it is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>76</mn><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mo> </mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> solution Hence volume strength <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>79</mn><mo>≃</mo><mn>10</mn></math> volumes</p>\n\n<p>Suggested Solution :- </p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mfenced><mrow><mi>aq</mi><mo>.</mo></mrow></mfenced></mrow></msub><mo>⟶</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℓ</mi></mfenced><mo>+</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi></mfenced></math></p>\n\n<p>The above equation is a classic example of<br />\ndisproportionation of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math>, hence we should take <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi></math>-factor of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mo> </mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> solution <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>≡</mo><mn>1</mn><mo> </mo><mi>Gm</mi></math> equivalent of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>≡</mo><mn>1</mn></math> mole of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math></p>\n\n<p>This will produce <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math> moles of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> gas at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mo>.</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi><mo>.</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mo>.</mo></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>×</mo><mn>22</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>11</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>ℓ</mi></math> of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> gas</p>\n\n<p>i.e. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mi>ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mo> </mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> solution gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>11</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>ℓ</mi></math> of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> gas at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mo>.</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi><mo>.</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mo>.</mo></math></p>\n\n<p>Hence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mo>≡</mo><mn>11</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>2</mn></math> 'vol' <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> solution </p>\n\n<p>In the given question it is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>79</mn><mo> </mo><msub><mi>NH</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> solution</p>\n\n<p>Hence, volume strength <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>79</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>11</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>≃</mo><mn>10</mn></math> volumes</p>\n\n<p>Suggested Answer is option (A)</p>","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2020 (SA)","_marksId":"614da4fcb70c2f882f10d747"},{"id":51249,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Hydrogen","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"When heated in air, brown copper powder turns black. This black powder would turn brown again when heated with","options":["$\\mathrm{CO}$","$\\mathrm{H}_{2}$","$\\mathrm{O}_{2}$","$\\mathrm{NH}_{3}$"],"answer":2,"solution":"$\\mathrm{H}_{2}$ is reducing agent. Copper reacts with $\\mathrm{O}_{2}$ to give black colour CuO compound","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2017 (5 Nov SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d21347934547bf86ff86"},{"id":51250,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Hydrogen","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"Acetic acid reacts with sodium metal at room temperature to produce","options":["$\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$","$\\mathrm{H}_{2}$","$\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$","$\\mathrm{CO}$"],"answer":1,"solution":"$2 \\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{COOH}+2 \\mathrm{Na} \\longrightarrow 2 \\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{COONa}+\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\uparrow$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2013 (SA)","_marksId":"6174d2135c34a8f55de0f874"},{"id":51251,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Hydrogen","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"Permanent hardness of water can be removed by ","options":["heating","treating with sodium acetate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mrow><msub><mi>CH</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mi>COONa</mi></mrow></mfenced></math>","treating with calcium hydrogen carbonate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Ca</mi><msub><mfenced><msub><mi>HCO</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mfenced><mn>2</mn></msub></math>","treatment with sodium hexametaphosphate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mrow><msub><mi>Na</mi><mn>6</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mn>6</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>18</mn></msub></mrow></mfenced></math>"],"answer":3,"solution":"<p>Sodium hexametaphosphate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mrow><msub><mi>Na</mi><mn>6</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>18</mn></msub></mrow></mfenced></math> (or Calgon) coverts <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>Ca</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup></math> and<br />\n<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>Mg</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup></math> ions in water (which  cause hardness) into a soluble complex. </p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>Na</mi><mn>6</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mn>6</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>18</mn></msub><mo>⟶</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>Na</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>Na</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mn>6</mn></msub><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>18</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>-</mo></mrow></msubsup></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>Ca</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>Na</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mn>6</mn></msub><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>18</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>-</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>⟶</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>Na</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>CaNa</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mn>6</mn></msub><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>18</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>-</mo></mrow></msubsup></math></p>","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2019 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"614da6f13e1b24ee8c96bdd9"},{"id":51252,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Hydrogen","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"$\\mathrm{KMnO}_{4}$ reacts with $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{2}$ in an acidicmedium. The number of moles of oxygen produced per mole of $\\mathrm{KMnO}_{4}$ is","options":["$2.5$","5","$1.25$","2"],"answer":0,"solution":"(no. of eq. $)_{\\mathrm{KMnO}_{4}}=(\\text { no. of eq. })_{\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{2}}\\Rightarrow 1 \\times 5=\\mathrm{x} \\times 2\\Rightarrow \\mathrm{x}=2.5$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2017 (5 Nov SA)","_marksId":"6174d213d4e64752fb282a1c"}]}